.TH CMP 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
cmp \- compare two files byte by byte
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmp
.RB [ \-l | \-s ]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cmp
compares
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
byte by byte from the beginning, until they differ or one or both end.
.PP
By default, at the first byte where the two files differ,
.B cmp
writes "file1 file2 differ: char N, line L" (both 1-based) to standard
output and exits 1. If the files are identical over the entire length
of the shorter one but one file is longer than the other,
.B cmp
instead writes "cmp: EOF on shorter after byte N, in line L" to
standard error and exits 1. If the files are wholly identical,
.B cmp
prints nothing and exits 0.
.PP
Once a difference has been found, the line number reported for any
further byte position is always counted from
.IR file1 's
own newline bytes, even though
.I file1
and
.I file2
may from that point on disagree about where their own lines end;
POSIX leaves this case unspecified, and this is a deliberate choice.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l
Instead of stopping at the first difference, compare the entire
length of the shorter file and report every differing byte: one line
each, giving its 1-based byte position (decimal) and the two files'
byte values there (each in octal). Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B \-s
Write nothing to standard output. Differences (and the EOF-on-shorter
condition) are reported through the exit status alone. Does not
suppress the diagnostic written when a file cannot be opened or read
at all, since that is a different condition from the files differing.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-l .
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.IR file1 ", " file2
The two files to compare. Either operand, but not usefully both, may
be
.B \-
for standard input.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
If both
.I file1
and
.I file2
are given as
.BR \- ,
both refer to the same standard input stream, read sequentially (not
the same bytes for both), which is unlikely to be a useful comparison.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The files are identical.
.TP
.B 1
The files differ, including the case where one file is identical to
the first part of the other but shorter.
.TP
.B >1
An error occurred: invalid usage, a
.I file
that could not be opened, or a read error partway through the
comparison.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diff (1)
